        "type": "text",
        "text": "{\"id\":\"3327d5f1-6e0b-45b9-bcde-bc1524faa801\",\"title\":\"Ideation & Product Rationale — SFN-17: Build MailFn as a reusable programmable mailbox function\",\"content\":\"# Ideation & Product Rationale\\n\\nBacklog issue: [SFN-17: Build MailFn as a reusable programmable mailbox function](<https://linear.app/21n/issue/SFN-17/build-mailfn-as-a-reusable-programmable-mailbox-function>)\\n\\n## Context and motivating problem\\n\\nRouter's humane-analysis workflow exposed a broader infrastructure gap: agents and automated tests sometimes need controlled email identities that can receive verification codes, magic links, onboarding messages, lifecycle communication, password resets, and deletion confirmations.\\n\\nA project-specific implementation would solve the immediate workflow but duplicate the same concerns in future products: address creation, DNS, inbound processing, MIME parsing, attachments, secret handling, retention, API access, and agent integrations.\\n\\nThe opportunity is to establish MailFn as a standalone Superfunctions capability that can be deployed and reused by Router and unrelated projects.\\n\\n## Target users and stakeholders\\n\\n* Product and QA engineers running signup and lifecycle workflows.\\n* Browser and coding agents that need controlled access to received email.\\n* Superfunctions maintainers defining shared package and deployment contracts.\\n* Application teams that need inbound-email automation without operating a mail server.\\n* Security and operations stakeholders responsible for authentication secrets, retention, domains, and abuse controls.\\n\\n## Primary use cases\\n\\n### Controlled product acquisition\\n\\n1. A workflow requests a stable or expiring MailFn address.\\n2. The address is supplied during product signup.\\n3. MailFn waits for a matching verification message.\\n4. The authorized agent reads the code or link.\\n5. Subsequent onboarding and lifecycle messages remain associated with the same test identity.\\n\\n### Automated end-to-end testing\\n\\n1. A test creates an expiring inbox with a run identifier.\\n2. The application sends a message to the address.\\n3. The test waits for a matching sender, subject, recipient, or timestamp.\\n4. The test asserts body content, links, headers, or attachments.\\n5. The inbox expires according to policy.\\n\\n### Durable agent mailbox\\n\\n1. A project provisions a long-lived inbox for an agent.\\n2. Incoming messages trigger a webhook or queue event.\\n3. The agent lists, reads, labels, or replies to authorized messages.\\n4. All actions use scoped credentials and produce audit events.\\n\\nThis is a later capability, not required for the initial release.\\n\\n### Application inbound processing\\n\\n1. A custom domain or address receives operational mail.\\n2. Routing rules select a handler.\\n3. MailFn exposes parsed content and attachments through an API or signed webhook.\\n4. The application performs its own domain-specific action.\\n\\n## Identity-provider considerations\\n\\nMailFn solves email receipt and recovery; it does not replace external identity providers.\\n\\n* When an identity provider permits an account backed by an existing email address, a MailFn address can receive verification and recovery messages.\\n* Provider-specific trusted devices, phone verification, passkeys, biometrics, or consent screens remain separate controlled steps.\\n* Privacy-relay addresses may forward into MailFn, but the workflow must preserve the relay identity as evidence.\\n* If a sanctioned identity path is unavailable, downstream analysis should report the authenticated surface as unavailable rather than infer it.\\n\\n## Ideas discussed\\n\\n### Proposal: one logical address per test identity\\n\\nDifferent product identities should normally receive distinct addresses for provenance, concurrency, password recovery, deletion, and lifecycle isolation. They do not require separately operated mailbox infrastructure; many virtual inboxes can share one MailFn deployment.\\n\\n### Proposal: Cloudflare-native initial deployment\\n\\nUse Email Routing and an Email Worker for inbound receipt, Queues for asynchronous processing, R2 for raw messages and attachments, and D1 for mailbox and message metadata.\\n\\n### Proposal: provider-neutral core\\n\\nExpose portable mailbox, message, storage, and transport contracts so Cloudflare is the first deployment adapter rather than a permanent API constraint.\\n\\n### Proposal: agent-native access\\n\\nProvide TypeScript SDK, CLI, and MCP tools. Agents should receive inbox-scoped, least-privilege credentials instead of organization-wide administrative secrets.\\n\\n### Proposal: inbound-first delivery\\n\\nThe initial value is receiving and inspecting mail. Sending and replying should compose through SendFn so delivery infrastructure, suppressions, and event semantics are not duplicated.\\n\\n## Desired outcomes and success measures\\n\\n* Router can complete authorized verification and lifecycle workflows using controlled identities.\\n* Another project can adopt MailFn without importing Router code.\\n* A test can create an inbox, trigger email, wait for the correct message, and retrieve its evidence deterministically.\\n* Concurrent workflows cannot read one another's inboxes.\\n* Authentication codes and message bodies do not appear in normal logs.\\n* Raw messages and attachments follow explicit retention and deletion policies.\\n* MailFn packages can be built, tested, and released independently inside Superfunctions.\\n* The initial design leaves room for durable inboxes and richer mailbox behavior without changing core identifiers.\\n\\n## Decisions and rationale\\n\\n### Decision: place MailFn in Superfunctions\\n\\nRationale: the capability is reusable infrastructure with shared SDK, storage, HTTP, security, CLI, MCP, testing, and release concerns. Keeping it inside Router would create the wrong ownership boundary.\\n\\n### Decision: distinguish MailFn from SendFn\\n\\n* MailFn: stateful mailbox identities, inbound receipt, stored messages, retrieval, waiting, threads, and mailbox lifecycle.\\n* SendFn: outbound email, SMS, push, templates, suppressions, delivery providers, and delivery events.\\n* MailFn replies should delegate transport to SendFn through an explicit adapter.\\n\\nRationale: this avoids two implementations of outbound delivery and keeps multi-channel notification semantics in SendFn.\\n\\n### Decision: distinguish MailFn from TestFn\\n\\nMailFn owns mail-domain primitives and assertions. TestFn may orchestrate MailFn during test runs but should not own mailbox infrastructure.\\n\\n### Decision: do not begin with a complete public mail platform\\n\\nRationale: arbitrary outbound delivery introduces materially different abuse, reputation, compliance, suppression, billing, and operations requirements. The first release should validate the reusable mailbox core.\\n\\n## Alternatives considered\\n\\n### Embed the capability in Router\\n\\nRejected for the shared capability. Router may consume MailFn through an adapter, but should not own its general implementation.\\n\\n### Use one shared address for every product\\n\\nNot preferred. It weakens provenance, makes concurrent OTP matching unsafe, complicates lifecycle evidence, and couples password resets and deletion tests.\\n\\n### Use plus-addresses as the primary identity mechanism\\n\\nNot preferred. Some products normalize or reject plus-addresses. MailFn may support them as optional aliases, while unique local parts remain the default.\\n\\n### Build complete inbound and outbound functionality immediately\\n\\nRejected for the initial phase. It increases security and operational risk before the core use case is validated.\\n\\n### Extend SendFn directly with mailbox state\\n\\nNot preferred. SendFn's current cross-channel delivery purpose does not naturally own inbound mailbox identity, storage, waiting, threading, or agent access.\\n\\n## Product and UX considerations\\n\\n* Inbox creation should return an address, opaque inbox identifier, scoped token, and expiry.\\n* Stable and expiring inboxes should be explicit choices.\\n* Wait operations need sender, recipient, subject, timestamp, unread-state, and content predicates.\\n* OTP and link extraction must identify the source message and avoid returning stale results.\\n* Messages should expose parsed text and HTML while preserving raw MIME for evidence.\\n* Human-readable CLI output must redact sensitive content by default.\\n* MCP tools should prefer narrow operations instead of returning entire mailboxes.\\n* Custom domains require clear DNS status and verification diagnostics.\\n* Deletion and expiry behavior must be predictable and observable.\\n\\n## Operational considerations\\n\\n* Reject unknown or inactive recipients.\\n* Do not log message bodies, links, codes, credentials, or attachment content.\\n* Apply quotas at project, inbox, and sender levels.\\n* Maintain metadata-only metrics for receipt, parse latency, wait latency, failures, and storage.\\n* Define retry and dead-letter behavior before relying on asynchronous processing.\\n* Make DNS and routing changes reversible.\\n* Keep public outbound disabled until separately reviewed and authorized.\\n\\n## Assumptions\\n\\n* Cloudflare remains the preferred initial runtime.\\n* Superfunctions next is the relevant integration baseline because it contains SendFn.\\n* A dedicated implementation worktree will be used.\\n* TypeScript is the initial SDK and runtime language.\\n* The first release can be internal or limited while contracts stabilize.\\n\\n## Goals\\n\\n* Reusable programmable inboxes.\\n* Deterministic verification-message retrieval.\\n* Provider-neutral contracts.\\n* Least-privilege agent access.\\n* Explicit MailFn and SendFn composition.\\n* Independent package release and validation.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n\\n* Immediate implementation from this ideation item.\\n* Public arbitrary-recipient outbound email.\\n* Full desktop or webmail UI.\\n* Phone or trusted-device automation.\\n* Replacing external OAuth identity providers.\\n* Staffing, scheduling, or commercial commitments.\\n\\n## Product acceptance criteria\\n\\n* MailFn can be explained without relying on Router-specific behavior.\\n* Every initial use case maps to an explicit API and security control.\\n* MailFn, SendFn, and TestFn responsibilities do not overlap ambiguously.\\n* The initial release has measurable success signals and clear non-goals.\\n* Later mailbox capabilities can be added without weakening first-release security boundaries.\\n\\n## Open questions\\n\\n* Should the public facade be `mailfn`, `@mailfn/core`, or both?\\n* Which features belong in `@mailfn/testing` rather than core?\\n* What default retention applies to stable inboxes, expiring inboxes, raw MIME, and attachments?\\n* Should extraction run in the service, SDK, MCP layer, or configurable combinations?\\n* Is the first release internal-only, private-package, or public-package?\\n* Which custom domain will be used for the first controlled deployment?\\n* What project and environment tenancy model is required?\\n* Which mailbox operations require immutable audit records?\\n* When should outbound reply support enter the roadmap?\",\"icon\":null,\"color\":null,\"url\":\"https://linear.app/21n/document/ideation-and-product-rationale-sfn-17-build-mailfn-as-a-reusable-a98720d95595\",\"slugId\":\"a98720d95595\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-08-10T08:01:41.239Z\",\"updatedAt\":\"2026-08-10T08:01:42.226Z\",\"archivedAt\":null,\"creator\":{\"id\":\"3a6ed003-6380-47ef-9e7b-b2b01ebddc6a\",\"name\":\"Aravind\"},\"updatedBy\":{\"id\":\"3a6ed003-6380-47ef-9e7b-b2b01ebddc6a\",\"name\":\"Aravind\"},\"project\":null,\"initiative\":null,\"team\":null,\"issue\":{\"id\":\"SFN-17\",\"title\":\"Build MailFn as a reusable programmable mailbox function\"}}"
      }
    ],
