        "type": "text",
        "text": "{\"id\":\"2a30481e-7e19-45a3-8c7c-2ae1e030dfc4\",\"title\":\"Architecture & Delivery Plan — SFN-17: Build MailFn as a reusable programmable mailbox function\",\"content\":\"# Architecture & Delivery Plan\\n\\nBacklog issue: [SFN-17: Build MailFn as a reusable programmable mailbox function](<https://linear.app/21n/issue/SFN-17/build-mailfn-as-a-reusable-programmable-mailbox-function>)\\n\\n## Current-state constraints\\n\\n* Superfunctions next contains SendFn for outbound email, SMS, push, templates, suppressions, and delivery events.\\n* The shared delivery contract currently models outbound delivery; it does not model inboxes or inbound receipt.\\n* No MailFn domain exists in the inspected worktrees.\\n* Superfunctions workspace and release targets are explicitly registered rather than discovered automatically.\\n* The relevant existing worktrees contain unrelated changes, so implementation must use a dedicated worktree.\\n* The initial Router use case needs inbound verification and lifecycle evidence, not a general outbound mail service.\\n* Email bodies, verification codes, magic links, and attachments are sensitive authentication data.\\n* Cloudflare Email Routing delivers incoming messages to a Worker but does not itself provide the complete MailFn storage and client API.\\n\\n## Proposed architecture\\n\\n```text\\nInternet sender\\n    |\\n    v\\nDedicated inbound domain\\n    |\\n    v\\nCloudflare Email Routing\\n    |\\n    v\\nMailFn Email Worker\\n    |-- validate recipient and limits\\n    |-- persist raw MIME safely\\n    |-- enqueue processing event\\n    v\\nCloudflare Queue\\n    |\\n    v\\nMIME parser and indexer\\n    |-- D1: projects, inboxes, messages, threads, tokens, events\\n    |-- R2: raw MIME and attachments\\n    |-- webhook/event dispatcher\\n    v\\nMailFn HTTP API\\n    |\\n    +-- TypeScript SDK\\n    +-- CLI\\n    +-- MCP server\\n    +-- project integrations\\n\\nOutbound send or reply\\n    |\\n    v\\nMailFn SendFn adapter\\n    |\\n    v\\nSendFn delivery provider\\n```\\n\\n## Component boundaries\\n\\n### `@mailfn/core`\\n\\nOwns provider-neutral types, validation, domain logic, and interfaces:\\n\\n* project and tenancy context;\\n* inbox lifecycle and addressing;\\n* message and attachment models;\\n* thread reconstruction;\\n* storage contracts;\\n* inbound transport contract;\\n* authorization and scoped credential contract;\\n* idempotency and event types;\\n* retention and deletion policies.\\n\\nIt must not import Cloudflare runtime types.\\n\\n### `@mailfn/cloudflare`\\n\\nOwns:\\n\\n* Email Worker entrypoint;\\n* HTTP Worker deployment;\\n* Email Routing integration;\\n* D1 migrations and storage adapter;\\n* R2 object adapter;\\n* Queue producer and consumer;\\n* Cloudflare-specific configuration and observability;\\n* DNS and custom-domain deployment guidance.\\n\\n### `@mailfn/client`\\n\\nOwns the TypeScript client for remote MailFn deployments:\\n\\n* inbox creation and lifecycle;\\n* list/read/wait message operations;\\n* attachment retrieval;\\n* thread and label operations when available;\\n* typed errors, pagination, retries, and cancellation.\\n\\n### `@mailfn/testing`\\n\\nOwns testing-oriented helpers:\\n\\n* expiring inbox fixtures;\\n* wait predicates;\\n* OTP extraction;\\n* verification-link extraction;\\n* header, body, and attachment assertions;\\n* adapters for test runners.\\n\\nExtraction must always return the source message identifier and received timestamp.\\n\\n### `@mailfn/mcp`\\n\\nOwns agent-facing tools:\\n\\n* create inbox;\\n* wait for message;\\n* list messages;\\n* read one message;\\n* extract verification material;\\n* revoke inbox.\\n\\nThe default agent credential must be scoped to one inbox and should not permit deletion, sending, domain administration, or token creation unless explicitly granted.\\n\\n### `@mailfn/sendfn`\\n\\nOwns MailFn-to-SendFn composition for:\\n\\n* new outbound messages;\\n* replies and forwarding;\\n* delivery-status correlation;\\n* suppression-aware send behavior.\\n\\nMailFn core must not duplicate provider-specific outbound delivery.\\n\\n### Optional facade\\n\\nA `mailfn` package may re-export the stable high-level client after the core API is proven. This remains an open packaging decision.\\n\\n## Initial data model\\n\\n### Project\\n\\n* `id`\\n* `slug`\\n* `display_name`\\n* `status`\\n* `default_retention_policy`\\n* `created_at`\\n* `updated_at`\\n\\n### Inbox\\n\\n* `id`\\n* `project_id`\\n* `address`\\n* `display_name`\\n* `kind`: stable or expiring\\n* `status`: active, disabled, expired, deleted\\n* `metadata`\\n* `expires_at`\\n* `created_at`\\n* `updated_at`\\n\\nAddress uniqueness must be enforced.\\n\\n### Credential\\n\\n* `id`\\n* `project_id`\\n* `inbox_id` when inbox-scoped\\n* `token_hash`\\n* `permissions`\\n* `expires_at`\\n* `last_used_at`\\n* `revoked_at`\\n* `created_at`\\n\\nOnly token hashes are stored.\\n\\n### Message\\n\\n* `id`\\n* `project_id`\\n* `inbox_id`\\n* `internet_message_id`\\n* `envelope_from`\\n* `envelope_to`\\n* `subject`\\n* `received_at`\\n* `parsed_at`\\n* `text_body`\\n* `html_body`\\n* `raw_object_key`\\n* `thread_id`\\n* `authentication_results`\\n* `size_bytes`\\n* `status`\\n* `retention_expires_at`\\n\\nA uniqueness constraint should cover the normalized provider delivery identifier and recipient.\\n\\n### Attachment\\n\\n* `id`\\n* `message_id`\\n* `filename`\\n* `content_type`\\n* `size_bytes`\\n* `object_key`\\n* `sha256`\\n* `created_at`\\n\\n### Thread\\n\\n* `id`\\n* `project_id`\\n* `inbox_id`\\n* `normalized_subject`\\n* `last_message_at`\\n* `created_at`\\n\\nThreading uses Message-ID, In-Reply-To, and References before subject fallback.\\n\\n### Webhook\\n\\n* `id`\\n* `project_id`\\n* `inbox_id` when scoped\\n* `url`\\n* `event_types`\\n* `secret_hash`\\n* `status`\\n* `created_at`\\n* `updated_at`\\n\\n### Audit event\\n\\n* `id`\\n* `project_id`\\n* `actor_type`\\n* `actor_id`\\n* `action`\\n* `resource_type`\\n* `resource_id`\\n* `metadata`\\n* `created_at`\\n\\nAudit metadata must not contain message bodies, codes, links, or credentials.\\n\\n## Initial API surface\\n\\n### Inbox lifecycle\\n\\n```http\\nPOST   /v1/inboxes\\nGET    /v1/inboxes/:inboxId\\nPATCH  /v1/inboxes/:inboxId\\nDELETE /v1/inboxes/:inboxId\\nPOST   /v1/inboxes/:inboxId/tokens\\nDELETE /v1/inboxes/:inboxId/tokens/:tokenId\\n```\\n\\nInbox creation accepts an optional requested local part, expiry, idempotency key, and metadata.\\n\\n### Messages\\n\\n```http\\nGET /v1/inboxes/:inboxId/messages\\nGET /v1/inboxes/:inboxId/messages/:messageId\\nGET /v1/inboxes/:inboxId/messages/:messageId/raw\\nGET /v1/inboxes/:inboxId/messages/:messageId/attachments/:attachmentId\\n```\\n\\nList operations support cursor pagination and filters for sender, recipient, subject, received-after, received-before, unread state, and thread.\\n\\n### Waiting and matching\\n\\n```http\\nPOST /v1/inboxes/:inboxId/messages/wait\\n```\\n\\nRequest fields may include:\\n\\n* `after`;\\n* timeout;\\n* sender or sender-domain;\\n* subject predicate;\\n* recipient;\\n* unread-only;\\n* text or HTML predicate;\\n* expected message count.\\n\\nThe response returns the exact matched message. Timeout is a normal typed result rather than an unclassified server error.\\n\\n### Verification helpers\\n\\n```http\\nPOST /v1/inboxes/:inboxId/messages/:messageId/extract\\n```\\n\\nThe initial extraction types are OTP and verification link. Extraction is deterministic, source-attributed, and does not mark unrelated messages as consumed.\\n\\n### Events\\n\\nInitial event types:\\n\\n* `message.received`\\n* `message.parsed`\\n* `message.parse_failed`\\n* `inbox.expiring`\\n* `inbox.expired`\\n* `webhook.delivery_failed`\\n\\nEvery event has a stable identifier and versioned payload schema.\\n\\n## Inbound data flow\\n\\n 1. Email Routing invokes the MailFn Email Worker with envelope metadata and raw MIME.\\n 2. The Worker normalizes the recipient and looks up an active inbox.\\n 3. Unknown, disabled, or expired recipients are rejected.\\n 4. Size and project quotas are checked before durable storage.\\n 5. Raw MIME is written to R2 under a non-guessable object key.\\n 6. A minimal pending message row is created in D1.\\n 7. A versioned parse event is sent to a Queue.\\n 8. The consumer parses MIME, stores normalized bodies and attachment metadata, and writes attachments to R2.\\n 9. D1 message state moves from pending to ready or parse-failed.\\n10. Signed webhooks and waiting clients are notified.\\n11. Retention jobs remove expired objects and rows according to policy.\\n\\nThe exact ordering and compensation behavior for R2, D1, and Queue failures must be specified before implementation.\\n\\n## Security and privacy\\n\\n### Authorization\\n\\n* Organization or deployment administrative credentials are never given to browser agents.\\n* Project credentials manage inboxes inside one project.\\n* Inbox credentials access only one inbox.\\n* Permissions are explicit booleans or named scopes.\\n* Tokens are shown once, stored hashed, revocable, and optionally expiring.\\n* Every API route validates both permission and resource ownership.\\n\\n### Authentication secrets\\n\\n* OTPs and verification links are treated as secrets.\\n* Normal logs contain identifiers and timing, not message bodies or extracted values.\\n* CLI output redacts body content unless explicitly requested.\\n* MCP results should return the minimum content required by the operation.\\n* Published audit artifacts must redact codes, links, tokens, and personal data.\\n\\n### Inbound controls\\n\\n* Reject unknown local parts instead of operating an unrestricted catch-all.\\n* Apply message-size, inbox, project, and sender limits.\\n* Record SPF, DKIM, and DMARC results where available.\\n* Sanitize rendered HTML and never execute message scripts.\\n* Do not fetch remote images during parsing or preview.\\n* Validate attachment content type and size independently of filename.\\n\\n### Webhooks\\n\\n* Sign payloads with per-webhook secrets.\\n* Include timestamp and delivery identifier.\\n* Require consumers to reject stale or replayed deliveries.\\n* Retry with bounded exponential backoff.\\n* Disable or quarantine repeatedly failing endpoints.\\n\\n### Outbound controls\\n\\nAny future public outbound capability requires:\\n\\n* verified sender domains;\\n* suppression handling;\\n* complaint and bounce processing;\\n* rate and volume limits;\\n* abuse detection;\\n* audit and operator controls;\\n* explicit production-security approval.\\n\\nThe initial MailFn release should not expose an open relay.\\n\\n## Reliability\\n\\n* Use idempotency keys for inbox creation and client retries.\\n* Deduplicate inbound deliveries before webhook dispatch.\\n* Separate receipt from parsing through a Queue.\\n* Define bounded retries and dead-letter handling.\\n* Preserve raw MIME when parsing fails, subject to retention.\\n* Make wait operations cancellation-aware and resilient to reconnects.\\n* Return typed retryability information in API errors.\\n* Test partial failures across R2, D1, Queue, and webhook boundaries.\\n* Provide explicit inbox and message state transitions.\\n\\n## Observability\\n\\nMetadata-only metrics:\\n\\n* inbound accepted and rejected counts;\\n* receipt and parse latency;\\n* parse failures;\\n* message and attachment sizes;\\n* wait duration and timeout count;\\n* webhook success, retry, and failure counts;\\n* storage volume;\\n* active and expired inbox counts;\\n* authorization failures and rate-limit events.\\n\\nStructured logs must use request, message, inbox, project, event, and delivery identifiers without sensitive content.\\n\\n## Migration and rollback\\n\\nThere is no existing MailFn data to migrate.\\n\\nInitial rollout should:\\n\\n* use a dedicated inbound subdomain;\\n* avoid changing primary organizational email MX records;\\n* isolate test and production environments;\\n* keep DNS records documented and reversible;\\n* preserve a forwarding fallback during early validation when appropriate;\\n* support disabling routing without deleting stored evidence;\\n* make D1 migrations forward-only with tested restoration from backups;\\n* avoid coupling Router directly to Cloudflare-specific types.\\n\\nRollback means disabling the MailFn route, revoking client credentials, stopping new receipt, and preserving or deleting stored messages according to the approved incident and retention policy.\\n\\n## Delivery phases\\n\\n### Phase 0: specification and threat model\\n\\n* Confirm package boundaries.\\n* Define API and schema versions.\\n* Resolve open product questions.\\n* Model threats and abuse cases.\\n* Define retention and data classification.\\n* Establish release and compatibility policy.\\n\\n### Phase 1: inbound test inbox\\n\\n* Core domain types and storage interfaces.\\n* Cloudflare Email Worker and routing.\\n* D1/R2/Queue persistence.\\n* Stable and expiring inboxes.\\n* List, read, and wait APIs.\\n* OTP and verification-link extraction.\\n* TypeScript client.\\n* Scoped inbox tokens.\\n* Focused integration and failure tests.\\n\\n### Phase 2: developer and agent surfaces\\n\\n* CLI.\\n* MCP server.\\n* Signed webhooks.\\n* Custom-domain administration.\\n* Test-runner integration.\\n* Retention automation and audit events.\\n* Operational dashboards and alerts.\\n\\n### Phase 3: durable mailbox behavior\\n\\n* Threads.\\n* Labels.\\n* Search.\\n* Drafts.\\n* Reply and forward through SendFn.\\n* Richer agent policy and delegation.\\n* Expanded lifecycle operations.\\n\\n### Phase 4: public-platform readiness\\n\\nOnly after explicit approval:\\n\\n* tenancy and quotas at public-service scale;\\n* domain onboarding and verification;\\n* outbound abuse and reputation controls;\\n* billing and support surfaces;\\n* compliance and data-residency requirements;\\n* protocol compatibility where justified.\\n\\n## Validation\\n\\n* Unit tests for address normalization, permissions, retention, extraction, and thread reconstruction.\\n* MIME fixture matrix covering text, HTML, multipart, encodings, malformed input, and attachments.\\n* Cloudflare integration tests for Email Worker, D1, R2, and Queue behavior.\\n* Authorization tests proving cross-project and cross-inbox isolation.\\n* Concurrent signup tests proving correct OTP attribution.\\n* Retry and idempotency tests.\\n* Webhook signature and replay tests.\\n* Retention and deletion verification.\\n* DNS and custom-domain smoke tests.\\n* Router consumer test without Cloudflare types leaking into Router.\\n* Package build, typecheck, test, pack, and install verification.\\n* Dedicated MailFn release gate before publication.\\n\\n## Rollout\\n\\n* Start with an isolated internal domain and project.\\n* Validate one Router workflow and one unrelated project integration.\\n* Compare actual receipt latency, parse reliability, extraction accuracy, storage, and operational burden against success thresholds defined during specification.\\n* Expand to additional internal projects only after isolation and retention checks pass.\\n* Add durable mailbox features incrementally.\\n* Keep arbitrary public outbound disabled until its separate gate passes.\\n\\n## Dependencies\\n\\n* Cloudflare Email Routing, Workers, D1, R2, and Queues.\\n* Superfunctions shared HTTP, error, storage, webhook, configuration, and observability patterns where suitable.\\n* SendFn and the shared delivery contract for outbound composition.\\n* TestFn integration for optional test orchestration.\\n* Explicit root workspace and release-target updates.\\n* DNS ownership for a dedicated inbound subdomain.\\n\\n## Technical risks and tradeoffs\\n\\n* Cloudflare-native deployment is operationally convenient but must not leak into the core contract.\\n* Distributed writes across R2, D1, and Queues require explicit compensation and reconciliation.\\n* Long-poll waiting is simple for clients but may need a more scalable event mechanism later.\\n* Storing parsed bodies improves queries but increases sensitive-data footprint.\\n* Raw MIME is valuable evidence but increases storage and deletion complexity.\\n* Aggressive expiration protects data but can erase lifecycle evidence prematurely.\\n* Custom domains improve identity control but add DNS and reputation operations.\\n* Rich mailbox functionality increases overlap risk unless SendFn boundaries remain enforced.\\n\\n## Assumptions\\n\\n* TypeScript is the first implementation language.\\n* Cloudflare is the first deployment adapter.\\n* The initial release is inbound-first.\\n* Router is an early consumer, not the owner.\\n* SendFn remains the outbound delivery authority.\\n* A dedicated worktree and scoped installation strategy will be used.\\n\\n## Open questions\\n\\n* Exact package names and public facade.\\n* First-release visibility and support expectations.\\n* Retention defaults by inbox kind and data type.\\n* Extraction placement and extensibility.\\n* Long-poll, webhook, or event-stream behavior for waiting clients.\\n* Project and environment tenancy model.\\n* Search indexing approach for sensitive message content.\\n* Backup and recovery expectations.\\n* Custom-domain provisioning workflow.\\n* Criteria for entering durable mailbox and public-platform phases.\",\"icon\":null,\"color\":null,\"url\":\"https://linear.app/21n/document/architecture-and-delivery-plan-sfn-17-build-mailfn-as-a-reusable-f9905e68e72e\",\"slugId\":\"f9905e68e72e\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-08-10T08:01:38.832Z\",\"updatedAt\":\"2026-08-10T08:01:40.249Z\",\"archivedAt\":null,\"creator\":{\"id\":\"3a6ed003-6380-47ef-9e7b-b2b01ebddc6a\",\"name\":\"Aravind\"},\"updatedBy\":{\"id\":\"3a6ed003-6380-47ef-9e7b-b2b01ebddc6a\",\"name\":\"Aravind\"},\"project\":null,\"initiative\":null,\"team\":null,\"issue\":{\"id\":\"SFN-17\",\"title\":\"Build MailFn as a reusable programmable mailbox function\"}}"
      }
    ],
    "isError": false
  }
}

